        ("CHAPTER 1", "INTRODUCTION", "The introduction establishes Digital Defense Hub as a multi-tenant defensive cybersecurity and digital-forensics platform. Its implemented purpose is to connect ransomware-oriented deception, security-event handling, evidence integrity and media-forensic analysis in one investigation environment.", "React console; Go API; PostgreSQL; Python AI engine; endpoint/file-event monitoring", "JWT/RBAC/tenant middleware; service/repository layering; evidence-first records", "A clear project context showing how user actions, events, scoring and evidence are connected."),
        ("1.1", "Background", "The background is the practical problem addressed by DDH: ransomware-related file activity can escalate quickly, while manipulated media can mislead an investigation if the original artifact and analysis context are not preserved. DDH uses protected deception resources and forensic workflows to provide early, explainable signals.", "canary/honeytoken resources; normalized file events; media artifact uploads; evidence records", "baseline hashing; event normalization; category-aware media handling; analyst review pathways", "Security and forensic records that preserve both detection context and original-artifact identity."),
        ("1.2", "Objectives", "The implemented objectives are to deploy/monitor decoy resources, score pre-encryption behavior, preserve evidence integrity, analyse supported media, and keep every workflow tenant-aware. The project deliberately joins these objectives through common incidents, evidence and notification records.", "deception deployment; pre-encryption request features; evidence manifests; media-analysis jobs; role-protected APIs", "input validation; SHA-256 identity; Ed25519 manifest verification; JWT/RBAC; worker processing", "Traceable detection, forensic and incident-response outputs for authorized organization users."),
        ("1.3", "Purpose", "The purpose is to provide an evidence-first defensive workflow instead of isolated tools. A canary trigger, high-entropy file burst or deepfake-analysis result can be retained with organization, event and evidence context and can later be reviewed through the platform.", "security events; protected resources; media artifacts; incidents; analyst requests", "correlation; risk explanations; evidence custody data; controlled response workflow", "An integrated security-operation record that supports investigation rather than only alert display."),
        ("1.3.1", "Scope", "The DDH scope includes identity/access controls, honeytoken/canary protection, event and correlation foundation, pre-encryption scoring, media forensics, evidence vault verification, notification and recovery workflow. The scope is limited to capabilities represented by the source and configured services.", "frontend, Go backend packages, PostgreSQL migrations, AI-engine routes and configured adapters", "tenant scoping; API contracts; asynchronous outbox/worker patterns; evidence validation", "A defined platform boundary showing what the project processes and how the components connect."),
        ("1.3.2", "Applicability", "The implemented project is applicable to organizations that need a shared interface for protected-file signals, security investigations and digital-media examination. Applicability is based on organization-scoped accounts, permissions, case/evidence records and dashboard workspaces, not on a claim that every external environment is automatically supported.", "organization users; role permissions; endpoints/events; files/media; incidents", "multi-tenancy; least privilege; authenticated requests; configurable external adapters", "Role-appropriate access to the organization’s relevant security and forensic data."),
        ("1.4", "Achievements", "The source repository contains a complete multi-component platform structure: React UI, Go API, PostgreSQL migrations, FastAPI scoring/media routes, evidence crypto operations, event/correlation packages and recovery workflow APIs. The achievement reported here is the integrated implementation and its explainable controls, not an unverified production-performance claim.", "implemented routes, scoring engines, evidence APIs, media analyzers, migrations and dashboard module mappings", "tests, DTO validation, hashing/signature checks, request-ID handling and health/metrics support", "A modular platform capable of presenting source-confirmed workflows and their traceable outputs."),
        ("1.5", "Organization of Report", "The report is organized so that the project problem and stack are introduced first, then requirements and design are described, followed by implementation/testing, result interpretation and conclusion. The expanded pages preserve that order and link every chapter to a concrete DDH module or control.", "chapter structure; diagrams; formula index; module descriptions; test cases", "clear separation of implemented facts, formula rules and known operational boundaries", "A report that can be read chapter by chapter without adding unsupported implementation claims."),
        ("CHAPTER 2", "SURVEY OF TECHNOLOGIES", "This chapter explains the technologies actually used in DDH and why each belongs to the implemented architecture. The system is not a single-language application: browser experience, security workflow, data persistence and AI/media analysis are separate concerns connected through API contracts.", "React/Vite/TypeScript; Go; PostgreSQL; Python/FastAPI; PyTorch; ONNX Runtime", "service authentication; tenant-aware API operations; scoped storage and job workspace handling", "A technology map that connects each runtime to a project responsibility."),
        ("CHAPTER 3", "REQUIREMENTS AND ANALYSIS", "Requirements and analysis describe the concrete records, controls and runtime interactions required for DDH. They are based on source components and are not presented as procurement specifications or benchmark guarantees.", "web/API/AI services; database; monitoring; evidence/media jobs; authorization context", "validation, persistence, queuing, scoring, audit context and controlled failure responses", "A consistent view of what data and services are necessary for each DDH workflow."),
        ("3.1", "Problem Definition", "The problem is that an organization needs earlier and more contextual handling of suspicious file behavior while also preserving digital evidence and analysing possible manipulated media. Ordinary file events can be noisy; DDH increases investigative value by linking event patterns to canaries/honeytokens, evidence and incident context.", "file activity; decoy interaction; uploaded media; threats; incidents; evidence", "multi-signal correlation; hash identity; tenant protection; analyst review; response approval", "An explainable security/forensic workflow rather than separate unconnected alerts."),
