        ("CHAPTER 4", "SYSTEM DESIGN", "System design separates presentation, API/control-plane, persistence, monitoring and AI processing. The separation allows each layer to use the same organization context and evidence discipline while retaining an implementation appropriate to its responsibility.", "React client; Go domain modules; database; AI engine; workers; monitoring input", "middleware, DTOs, services, repositories, external adapter boundaries and audits", "A layered architecture that supports extensible security workflows without duplicating authorization or persistence logic."),
        ("4.1", "Basic Modules", "The basic DDH modules are identity/access, honeytoken and canary protection, pre-encryption detection, media forensics, evidence vault, security-event correlation, incidents/notifications and recovery. The earlier detailed module pages explain seven core groups; this page places them in the overall design.", "module routes, service operations, stored entities and protected UI workspaces", "module-specific permissions plus shared authentication, tenant isolation and audit context", "Connected but independently maintainable security and forensic capabilities."),
        ("4.2", "Database Design", "PostgreSQL is the persistent layer for organizations, users, roles, sessions, events, threats, incidents, evidence, manifests, verification records, jobs and workflow states. Migrations create the data foundations incrementally, including normalized event/outbox/DLQ structures, correlation records, evidence crypto data and recovery tables.", "tenant identifiers, UUIDs, timestamps, status fields, hashes and relationship keys", "tenant-scoped queries; database transactions; unique constraints; migration versioning; worker-safe claims", "Durable records that preserve workflow state and support safe reprocessing or investigation."),
        ("4.2.1", "Schema Design", "Schema design represents the project’s domain entities and their relationships. Evidence manifests include evidence SHA-256, previous-manifest SHA-256, signing key and signature data. Event/correlation tables retain event identities and relationships. Analysis and training paths retain job, model and result metadata.", "entity fields, UUID identifiers, organization IDs, hash/signature data, timestamps and lifecycle states", "foreign/relationship references, unique identities, validation at API boundaries and migration-controlled changes", "A schema that supports traceability from an object or event to its verification and incident context."),
        ("4.2.2", "Data Integrity and Constraints", "Data integrity is enforced through multiple layers. API schemas reject malformed requests, tenant-scoped access constrains records to an organization, SHA-256 validates byte identity, Ed25519 validates manifest signatures and database constraints/idempotency avoid duplicate processing. Integrity checks are separate from risk decisions so a failed hash, signature or chain can be reported accurately.", "observed SHA-256, stored manifest, signature key, previous manifest, request/event identity", "object-hash comparison; signature verification; manifest-chain check; constraints and worker-safe transaction rules", "Verification results such as object_hash_verified, signature_verified and chain_verified, plus a recorded audit event.", "Evidence formula: H = SHA-256(B). Verification compares Hobserved with Hmanifest. A stored-manifest verification succeeds only when object hash, manifest signature and previous-manifest chain checks all succeed."),
        ("4.3", "Procedural Designs", "Procedural design describes how a request progresses through DDH: route registration, middleware, request validation, service logic, repository persistence and response mapping. Worker procedures claim queued work, process it, persist result/audit information and retry or dead-letter failures according to the module’s rules.", "HTTP request, service token, request ID, tenant/permission context, DTO fields and workflow state", "middleware checks; transactional persistence; retry; dead-letter; structured errors and logs", "A predictable procedure for normal, rejected and failed requests."),
        ("4.3.1", "Logic Diagrams", "The report diagrams represent implemented flow relationships: UI to API to database/AI engine; canary creation to hashing to monitoring; pre-encryption features to scoring to actions; media validation to analysis; evidence manifest to hash/signature/chain checks. They are flow explanations, not claims of hidden algorithms.", "validated requests, security events, protected files, evidence/media artifacts", "layered service flow, asynchronous worker path and scoring/verification decisions", "Visual trace from input through controlled processing to result/evidence output."),
        ("4.3.2", "Data Structures", "The project uses typed Go DTO/domain records and Pydantic Python schemas. Core structures include incident-risk features, pre-encryption features, risk factors, media-analysis request/response objects, evidence manifests, custody events and normalized security events. Typed fields make validation and response contracts explicit.", "UUIDs, strings, integers, percentages, booleans, timestamps, lists of factors/signals", "schema validation; strict organization/job/request identity; bounded values where defined; serialization", "Structured data that can be persisted, sent through APIs and displayed without guessing field meaning."),
        ("4.3.3", "Algorithms Design", "DDH uses deterministic algorithms where code defines them: weighted pre-encryption and incident scoring, SHA-256 hashing, Ed25519 signing/verification, manifest/custody chaining and image-specialist disagreement handling. It does not claim undocumented neural-network internals, OCR formulas or an unsupported universal deepfake rule.", "feature scores, weights, raw artifact bytes, manifest data, signature keys and analyzer results", "clamping, weighted sums, correlation bonuses, policy floors, logistic probability and cryptographic verification", "Explainable score factors, classifications, integrity status and review-oriented forensic results.", "Pre-encryption combined score = 0.45 × rule_score + 0.55 × ai_score, then clamped and raised by policy floor when required. Threat probability = 100 / (1 + exp(−(score − 50) / 10))."),
        ("4.4", "User Interface Design", "The React user interface provides a session shell, authentication navigation, dashboard/layout and generic module workspaces. Module mappings identify live API-backed records and actions. The UI is a presentation layer; authorization decisions remain enforced by the Go backend and its permission middleware.", "user session state, module route, API result, permitted action and organization context", "API client authentication; protected navigation; responsive layout; backend authorization for all sensitive records", "Role-appropriate presentation of live threat, incident, notification, evidence and analysis data."),
        ("4.5", "Security Issues", "DDH addresses security at request, data and workflow layers. The AI service requires a service token, requires matching X-Request-ID header/body UUIDs for scoring endpoints, limits request size, returns no-store/nosniff headers and avoids public OpenAPI/docs routes. Backend modules add JWT/RBAC/tenant checks and evidence operations use hashes/signatures.", "service token, request header, request UUID, organization/permission context, evidence artifact", "authentication, request-size validation, header/body request ID match, tenant isolation, signature/hash validation and audit logging", "Rejected malformed/unauthorized requests or a traceable protected workflow result."),
