        ("CHAPTER 5", "IMPLEMENTATION AND TESTING", "Implementation brings the design into source packages and routes; testing checks the intended behavior without overstating operational deployment. The project contains frontend, Go backend, Python engine, migrations and documentation/runbook assets that correspond to the architecture described earlier.", "source modules, configurations, migrations, unit/API tests and local runtime services", "build/type checks, service validation, protected APIs, worker behavior and test assertions", "A reproducible project implementation that can be inspected by component and workflow."),
        ("5.1", "Implementation Approaches", "The implementation uses a modular monolith Go backend with external AI/sandbox/recovery adapters. The handler-service-repository pattern separates HTTP validation, business logic and PostgreSQL access. Python FastAPI provides narrow AI endpoints while React provides operator interactions.", "HTTP routes, DTOs, service constructors, repositories, database pool and AI client calls", "dependency injection/wiring, REST response envelope, async workers and external-service token boundaries", "Maintainable modules that share security controls without duplicating them."),
        ("5.2", "Coding Details and Code Efficiency", "Code efficiency is supported through bounded queries, PostgreSQL pools/indexes, typed requests, workers for long-running work and atomic queue claims where configured. The scoring engine uses straightforward deterministic arithmetic and returns factor details rather than expensive opaque inference for ransomware pre-encryption assessment.", "typed DTOs/schemas, database queries, event batches, score features and worker jobs", "input constraints, clamping, idempotency, transaction handling, retries and result persistence", "Consistent API behavior with explainable processing cost and controlled asynchronous work."),
        ("5.2.1", "Code Efficiency", "The AI engine calculates only the factors required by the request and clamps scores to a 0–100 range. Media work is split into jobs and scoped workspaces so video/audio/image processing does not block ordinary API handling. The backend keeps ordinary request work separate from queues/workers where retries or external operations are required.", "risk features, file/media artifacts, job identifiers, runtime configuration and database connections", "scoped workspaces, job dispatch, worker retry, bounded operations and structured error responses", "A completed assessment/result or a clear error without silently consuming invalid or mismatched input."),
        ("5.3", "Testing Approach", "The testing approach combines unit-level scoring/crypto behavior, API request validation and workflow contracts. Pre-encryption inputs can be constructed deterministically to verify exact weights, floors and classifications. Evidence test cases can use known hash/signature values. API tests can verify token, request-ID and tenant/permission behavior.", "known inputs, expected values, controlled mock/development records and invalid variants", "deterministic assertions, negative tests, contract tests and regression coverage", "Confidence that an implemented change preserves expected secure behavior."),
        ("5.3.1", "Unit Testing", "Unit testing isolates a function or component. Suitable DDH unit targets include score-factor methods, SHA-256/canonical-manifest behavior, Ed25519 signature verification, policy-floor selection, media request compatibility and state transition validation. The aim is exact repeatable behavior with small known inputs.", "single function inputs such as features, hashes, manifest fields or request types", "direct function invocation and exact assertion of scores, flags, exceptions or returned objects", "Fast confirmation that a local rule or integrity function works before integration testing."),
        ("5.3.2", "Integrated Testing", "Integrated testing checks component connections: React/API behavior where configured, Go service/repository/database flow, Go-to-FastAPI scoring request handling, event/worker processing, and evidence-manifest persistence/verification. It is especially important because DDH integrates multiple runtimes and security contexts.", "authenticated requests, database-backed records, service tokens, queued work and evidence/media fixtures", "end-to-end contract validation, tenant/permission checks, persisted outcome assertions and failure-path checks", "Confirmation that the same request context and evidence identity survive across component boundaries."),
        ("5.4", "Modifications and Improvements", "Implemented improvement patterns include versioned events/rules/models/manifests, policy floors for strong ransomware indicators, specialist media model routing, event retries/DLQ and evidence integrity health checks. Future improvements remain future work until implemented and validated, especially external provider deployment and new model evaluation.", "version records, feature flags/configuration, test evidence and operational findings", "controlled migration, regression tests, rollback-aware workflow, explicit audit history", "A safe path to evolve the platform while preserving explanation and evidence traceability."),
        ("CHAPTER 6", "RESULTS AND DISCUSSION", "Results in this project are workflow outputs, not unsupported benchmark claims. The platform can return deployment state, normalized events, scoring factors/classification, media analysis responses, evidence verification states and incident/notification/recovery records. Discussion interprets these outputs in their correct context and limitations.", "requests/events/artifacts and their persisted context", "score explanation, verification separation, category-aware media handling and analyst review", "Inspectable results that show what was processed and which control or factor affected the result."),
        ("6.1", "Test Reports", "A DDH test report should identify the feature/input, expected result, observed result and security boundary being checked. For pre-encryption scoring, the report can verify factor weights, correlation bonus cap, policy floor and classification. For evidence, it can verify object hash, signature and chain independently. For media, it can verify request compatibility and hash/size validation before analyzer dispatch.", "test ID, fixture/request, expected status/score, observed result and logs", "repeatable automated tests, structured error codes and persisted verification/audit information", "A test record that demonstrates expected behavior without claiming unmeasured production effectiveness."),
        ("6.2", "User Documentation", "User documentation explains how authorized users interact with the platform: sign in, complete required authentication steps, open permitted modules, review alert/evidence context, submit media for supported analysis, verify evidence, and use response workflows only with required approval. Documentation must also distinguish demo UI records from live backend records.", "authorized account, organization context, module/workspace, input artifact or event and permitted action", "RBAC, tenant filtering, safe validation messages, linked records and approval states", "A practical operational guide that helps a user understand the significance and boundary of each displayed result."),
        ("CHAPTER 7", "CONCLUSIONS", "The conclusion is that DDH provides an integrated, evidence-oriented architecture for ransomware-related file behavior and digital-media forensic workflows. Its source-confirmed value is the connection between deception signals, deterministic scoring, media analysis, evidence integrity and tenant-aware operations.", "all implemented modules and their shared identity/evidence/authorization context", "explainability, cryptographic verification, workflow controls and analyst review", "A defensible summary of the implemented project rather than an exaggerated universal security claim."),
